Trial patch (item 2): frequency-aware admission for the MoE slot runtime —
pin the prewarm set, and LFU admission with a probationary segment.

Everything here is confined to src/llama-moe-slot.cpp, and inside it to
victim selection and install bookkeeping in process() plus the state
those need. No math, no graph code, no read path changes: the policy
decides only which experts are resident, never what is computed, so
greedy output is bit-identical with the policies on or off (the trial
checks this on device via token-id equality across arms).

Two policies, both env-gated and both default OFF:

  LLAMA_MOE_PIN_PREWARM=1 (2a)
    The prewarm table's experts become eviction-protected in process()'s
    victim scan (a keep[]-style skip in the policy scan's early passes).
    If every candidate in a layer's window is protected, the last pass
    falls back to plain LRU across all candidates — an install is never
    refused. The protected set is exactly the prewarm table (16 experts
    per layer in the trial's tables), built from prewarm_table at init;
    with no table the policy is a no-op.

  LLAMA_MOE_LFU_ADMIT=1 (2b)
    One demand counter per (layer, expert), incremented once per unique
    expert per window-mode process() call (demand-set semantics: a
    4-row DSpark verify batch counts its union once, not per row), and
    aged by halving all counters every 1,024 demand events.
    A miss always installs and always computes — LES has no second
    resident copy of an expert to compute from, so Avalon's literal
    "install only if the count beats the victim" cannot refuse here.
    Instead the install lands in a probationary class (quota: 2 slots
    per layer window) unless the missed expert's counter strictly
    beats the weakest general resident's counter, in which case it
    installs as a general resident directly. Victim selection prefers
    the probationary class (oldest first), then general residents by
    lowest counter (LRU breaks ties); a segment that yields no victim
    (all requested this call) falls through to the other, so the quota
    is a steady-state cap, not a wall. A probationary resident demanded
    again is promoted to general. Prewarm- and prefetch-published
    residents start as general.

  LFU crash fix (trial build 25, after build 24's LFU arm aborted on
  its first verify step with "layer 18 requires more than 32 unique
  experts in one graph"): two departures from stock could strand an
  install on a graph that fits the window, and both are removed.
  (a) A probationary install whose class quota was already full used
  to skip the free-slot scan and go straight to victim selection;
  installs now always scan free slots first, exactly like stock, and
  the quota is enforced by the victim scan's class preference once
  the window is actually full. (b) Every pass of the policy victim
  scan was class-filtered, so it could return no victim while a
  non-requested resident existed; the scan now ends with a final
  class-blind pass that picks the plain-LRU resident, excluding only
  this call's requested[] set and pending slots, exactly stock's
  last resort. Together these restore stock's fit guarantee: any
  graph whose unique experts per layer fit the window always
  places, and the guard fires only when a graph genuinely exceeds
  the window. Nothing else about the policies changes.

With both flags off, process() runs the original free-slot scan and
the original two-pass LRU eviction verbatim (the policy code is
branched around, not woven through), and the patched runtime must
reproduce the unpatched counters exactly — that is the trial's
device-side regression check for this patch.

Applied by scripts/prepare_v47.py AFTER ggml-cpu-alloc-only-buft.patch;
the two patches touch disjoint regions of this file (that patch adds
lookahead-accuracy telemetry around the prediction block; this one
works around the allocation loop), and either order applies cleanly.


